---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Field Notes — GeoSmart" description="Field notes, geological explanations and learning notes from Camila Fernández on geology, Norway, subsurface and scientific curiosity.">
  <section class="page-hero notes-hero"><div class="notes-scribble" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div class="container"><div class="section-kicker">Geology · Fieldwork · Cryosphere</div><h1>Field Notes</h1><p class="muted">Selected geological work, field observations and the questions behind how I read landscapes and the subsurface.</p><div class="notes-compass" aria-hidden="true"><span>N</span><b>+</b><span>E</span></div></div></section>

  <section class="section field-notes-page"><div class="container field-notes-layout">
    <article class="note-feature note-article-card">
      <div class="note-number">FIELD NOTE 01</div>
      <div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Glacial geomorphology</span><span>·</span><span>Greenland + Pyrenees</span></div>
      <h2>Tracing Deglaciation: Zackenberg Valley &amp; Aneto Glacier</h2>
      <p class="lead">A geomorphological and geochronological study connecting glacial landforms, ice-flow mechanics and glacier retreat to reconstruct how landscapes respond to changing ice cover.</p>

      <div class="article-teaser">
        <span>PROJECT SNAPSHOT</span>
        <p><strong>What I investigated</strong><br />Deglaciation phases in the Zackenberg Valley, NE Greenland, and glacier change in the Aneto Cirque, Pyrenees.</p>
        <p><strong>Methods</strong><br />Digital geomorphological mapping · GIS &amp; remote sensing · <sup>10</sup>Be exposure dating · photogrammetry · ice rheology</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-findings">
        <span>KEY FINDINGS</span>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>39 samples</strong> were inventoried across moraines, erratics and polished bedrock for cosmogenic exposure dating.</li>
          <li><strong>Four deglaciation phases</strong> were reconstructed in the Zackenberg Valley, from approximately 50–80 ka to the Early Holocene.</li>
          <li>The Aneto glacier lost <strong>64.7% of its surface area</strong> between 1981 and 2022, from 135.7 ha to 48.1 ha.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="note-takeaway"><span>GEOLOGICAL TAKEAWAY</span><p>Combining spatial mapping with <sup>10</sup>Be geochronology provides a way to connect the shape of a glacial landscape with the timing of its evolution, while modern remote sensing reveals the continuing response of alpine glaciers to changing mass balance.</p></div>

      <div class="note-skills"><span>SKILLS DEMONSTRATED</span><div class="project-tags"><li>Geomorphological mapping</li><li>GIS &amp; remote sensing</li><li>Cosmogenic dating</li><li>Photogrammetry</li><li>Ice rheology</li></div></div>

      <div class="note-footer"><span>Arctic &amp; alpine glacial systems</span><span>Field · Mapping · Geochronology</span></div>
    </article>

    <article class="note-feature note-article-card note-feature-secondary">
      <div class="note-number">FIELD NOTE 02</div>
      <div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Global tectonics</span><span>·</span><span>Geodynamics · Subduction</span></div>
      <h2>How Plate Tectonics Works: Forces, Rheology &amp; Deformation</h2>
      <p class="lead">A synthesis of global geodynamics exploring how plate-driving forces, lithospheric rheology and subduction geometry shape deformation from oceanic trenches to continental mountain belts.</p>
      <div class="article-teaser">
        <span>PROJECT SNAPSHOT</span>
        <p><strong>What I investigated</strong><br />Plate dynamics, mantle–lithosphere coupling, transform deformation, continental collision, intraplate uplift and subduction styles across global tectonic settings.</p>
        <p><strong>Methods &amp; frameworks</strong><br />Seismic tomography · plate kinematics · GPS/geodesy · focal mechanisms · balanced cross-sections · thermomechanical models · geotherms</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note-findings">
        <span>KEY FINDINGS</span>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Slab pull</strong> and gravitational edge forces provide major contributions to global plate motion, while mantle drag is not sufficient on its own to explain observed kinematics.</li>
          <li><strong>Subduction geometry matters:</strong> buoyant ridges can promote flat-slab subduction, suppress arc magmatism and transfer compression far into continental interiors.</li>
          <li><strong>Oblique convergence</strong> produces complex transpressional and transtensional systems rather than simple strike-slip motion, as illustrated by the Alpine Fault and San Andreas system.</li>
          <li><strong>Thermal and rheological structure</strong> strongly influence how crust deforms during collision, extension and metamorphism.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="note-takeaway"><span>GEOLOGICAL TAKEAWAY</span><p>Plate tectonics is an integrated thermo-mechanical system: the geometry, temperature and density of the lithosphere influence where forces are transmitted and how deformation is expressed at the surface.</p></div>
      <div class="note-skills"><span>SKILLS DEMONSTRATED</span><div class="project-tags"><li>Geodynamic interpretation</li><li>Plate kinematics</li><li>Structural analysis</li><li>Seismotectonics</li><li>Thermal modelling</li></div></div>
      <div class="note-footer"><span>Global tectonic systems</span><span>Geodynamics · Structure · Seismotectonics</span></div>
    </article>

    <article class="note-feature note-article-card note-feature-tertiary">
      <div class="note-number">FIELD NOTE 03</div>
      <div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Metamorphic field geology</span><span>·</span><span>Central System · Spain</span></div>
      <h2>Mapping a Metamorphic Terrane: Prádena &amp; Berzosa</h2>
      <p class="lead">A regional-to-detailed-scale study of the Eastern Guadarrama, combining 1:50,000 geological synthesis with 1:10,000 field cartography to understand lithology, metamorphic zonation, deformation and the evolution of the Variscan basement.</p>

      <div class="article-teaser">
        <span>PROJECT SNAPSHOT</span>
        <p><strong>What I investigated</strong><br />The tectonometamorphic architecture of the Prádena sheet and the Berzosa shear-zone area, from regional geological relationships to detailed field-scale structures and mineral zones.</p>
        <p><strong>Methods &amp; tools</strong><br />1:50,000 &amp; 1:10,000 geological mapping · structural measurements · thin-section petrography · stereographic projections · foliation trajectories · S–C fabrics · mobile GIS · geochemical &amp; geochronological interpretation</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-findings">
        <span>KEY FINDINGS</span>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Metamorphic zonation</strong> records a progressive Barrovian succession from lower-grade mineral assemblages toward high-grade sillimanite + K-feldspar zones and anatectic migmatites.</li>
          <li><strong>Polyphase deformation</strong> is expressed through successive ductile shearing, folding and later brittle–ductile structures, with major shear zones localising strain within the Variscan basement.</li>
          <li><strong>Scale changes interpretation:</strong> regional mapping reveals the architecture and geological history of the terrane, while detailed 1:10,000 cartography resolves foliation, fold geometry, shear fabrics and metamorphic boundaries in the field.</li>
          <li>The integrated framework links <strong>crustal thickening, metamorphic heating, partial melting and later exhumation/extension</strong> during the Variscan orogenic cycle, followed by younger Alpine deformation and uplift.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="note-takeaway"><span>GEOLOGICAL TAKEAWAY</span><p>Metamorphic terrains become much more interpretable when lithology, mineral assemblages and structures are mapped together. Working from regional synthesis to detailed field observations makes it possible to connect centimetre-scale fabrics with the tectonic evolution of an entire crustal domain.</p></div>

      <div class="note-skills"><span>SKILLS DEMONSTRATED</span><div class="project-tags"><li>Metamorphic field mapping</li><li>Structural geology</li><li>Petrographic interpretation</li><li>Stereographic analysis</li><li>Mobile GIS</li><li>Geological cross-sections</li></div></div>

      <div class="note-footer"><span>Variscan Central System</span><span>Mapping · Metamorphism · Structure</span></div>
    </article>

    <div class="note-list note-grid">
      <article class="note note-card note-green"><div class="note-index">04</div><div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Norway</span><span>Field · Trondheim</span></div><h3>Geology in Trondheim</h3><p>What changes when you start looking at an everyday landscape with a geologist's eye — and what I am noticing around Trondheim.</p><div class="note-footer"><span>Landscape</span><span>In preparation</span></div></article>
      <article class="note note-card note-orange"><div class="note-index">05</div><div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Sedimentology</span><span>Learning</span></div><h3>Reading a sedimentary log</h3><p>How observations become a sedimentary interpretation, and why the geological story is rarely as simple as one neat answer.</p><div class="note-footer"><span>Earth history</span><span>In preparation</span></div></article>
      <article class="note note-card note-ochre"><div class="note-index">06</div><div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Energy</span><span>Subsurface</span></div><h3>What makes a good reservoir?</h3><p>Porosity, permeability and the geological history behind reservoir quality — without drowning in jargon.</p><div class="note-footer"><span>Reservoirs</span><span>In preparation</span></div></article>
      <article class="note note-card note-cream"><div class="note-index">07</div><div class="note-meta"><span class="card-tag">Learning</span><span>Student life</span></div><h3>Things I wish I knew before studying geology</h3><p>Fieldwork, maps, software, mistakes and the gradual process of learning how to think like a geologist.</p><div class="note-footer"><span>Student geology</span><span>In preparation</span></div></article>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section field-trip-section"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="section-kicker">Field trips</div><h2>Learning from the landscape</h2></div><p class="muted">Field observations are where geological concepts become visible, measurable and connected to place.</p></div><article class="field-trip-card"><div class="field-trip-card-image" aria-hidden="true"><span>FIELD TRIP / SEPÚLVEDA</span><b>KARST GEOMORPHOLOGY</b></div><div class="field-trip-card-body"><div class="card-tag">Geomorphology · Karst landscapes</div><h3>Karst Geomorphology in the Duratón Canyon</h3><p>A field study of the karst morphologies observed in the Natural Park of the Duratón River near Sepúlveda, connecting direct observations with the geological and geomorphological context of the canyon.</p><p>The memory documents sinkholes, gorges, caves, crests, lapiaz and cross-bedding, interpreting how carbonate dissolution, fluvial erosion, fractures and stratification shape the landscape.</p><ul class="project-tags"><li>Field geology</li><li>Geomorphology</li><li>Karst landscapes</li><li>Carbonate rocks</li><li>Fluvial processes</li></ul><div class="project-actions"><a class="btn" href="/documents/Memoria_Campo-Sep%C3%BAlveda-CamilaF.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View project</a><a class="text-link dark" href="/documents/Memoria_Campo-Sep%C3%BAlveda-CamilaF.pdf" download="Memoria_Campo-Sepúlveda-CamilaF.pdf">Read report</a></div></div></article></div></section>

  <section class="notes-bottom"><div class="container"><span class="section-kicker">Keep looking closer</span><p>Some rocks tell stories in layers. Some landscapes tell them in relief. These notes are my way of paying attention.</p><span class="geology-line" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></section>
</BaseLayout>

